      <span class="article-dateline__item">
        {% set author = authors[authorId] %}
        By <a href="{{ author.url }}">{{ author.name }}</a><svg class="verified-badge" aria-label="Verified author" title="Verified" viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="14" height="14" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><circle cx="10" cy="10" r="10" fill="#1d9bf0"/><path d="M6 10.5l2.5 2.5L14 7.5" stroke="white" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>, {{ author.credentials }}
      </span>
    </div>

    <div class="blog-article__feature">
      <img src="/assets/images/blog/stamp-duty-australia-2026-state-by-state-guide-feature.jpg" alt="Stamp duty Australia 2026 — all states compared: what you pay in NSW, VIC, QLD, WA, SA, TAS, ACT and NT" width="1200" height="630" loading="eager">
    </div>

    <p class="blog-article__intro">Buying a property in Australia comes with one cost that surprises almost every first-time buyer: stamp duty. It is not hidden, but it is large — and because it is paid upfront at settlement, it can derail even the most carefully planned purchase budget. Stamp duty typically adds $15,000 to $50,000 on top of the purchase price for a median-priced property, depending on the state and the buyer's circumstances.</p>

    <p>This guide covers stamp duty across all eight Australian states and territories in 2026. You will find out exactly how it is calculated, what you will pay at common purchase prices, and which concessions can legitimately reduce or eliminate your bill — particularly if you are a first home buyer.</p>

    <p>Before you read on, use CalcPhi's free <a href="/australia/stamp-duty-calculator/">Stamp Duty Calculator</a> to get an instant, state-specific figure for your property. It covers all eight jurisdictions, first home buyer concessions, and foreign buyer surcharges — no sign-up needed.</p>


    <h2>What Is Stamp Duty and How Does It Work?</h2>
    <p>Stamp duty — officially called transfer duty or conveyance duty in most states — is a state and territory government tax charged every time ownership of real estate changes hands. The buyer pays it, not the seller. It is calculated on the property's purchase price or market value, whichever is higher, using a tiered progressive structure similar to income tax brackets. That means only the portion of the price within each bracket is taxed at that bracket's rate, not the full purchase price.</p>
    <p>Payment is typically required within 30 days of settlement. Miss that deadline and you can face interest charges and penalties from your state revenue office.</p>
    <p>Because each state and territory runs its own stamp duty regime independently, rates, thresholds, and exemptions vary enormously across the country. Victoria currently has the highest top-bracket rate in Australia; Queensland is generally the most affordable for mid-range properties. Understanding these differences matters — especially if you are deciding between markets or comparing the true upfront cost of buying interstate.</p>


    <h2>New South Wales</h2>
    <p>New South Wales applies a progressive transfer duty scale. The first $14,000 of value is taxed at 1.25%, the scale continues progressively up to 7% for the portion above $3,000,000 (the premium property duty threshold).</p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Purchase Price</th>
          <th>Standard Duty (approx.)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$17,990</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$750,000</td><td>$29,240</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$900,000</td><td>$35,835</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,000,000</td><td>$39,412</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,500,000</td><td>$64,412</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>NSW first home buyers receive a full stamp duty exemption on homes (new or established) valued up to $800,000, with a sliding concession applying between $800,000 and $1,000,000. Above $1,000,000, full duty applies regardless of buyer status.</p>
    <p>NSW also offers the First Home Buyer Choice scheme, which allows eligible buyers to opt for an annual property tax instead of lump-sum transfer duty at settlement. This can meaningfully reduce the upfront cash needed to buy. If you are buying in Sydney, use CalcPhi's <a href="/australia/first-home-buyer-calculator/">First Home Buyer Calculator</a> to model both options side by side.</p>


    <h2>Victoria</h2>
    <p>Victoria has the highest top-bracket stamp duty rate of any Australian state. Rates step up across five brackets from nil under $25,000 to 6.5% on the portion above $870,000 — the highest top-bracket rate in any Australian state.</p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Purchase Price</th>
          <th>Standard Duty (approx.)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$21,970</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$700,000</td><td>$37,070</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$900,000</td><td>$49,070</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,000,000</td><td>$55,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,500,000</td><td>$87,500</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Victorian first home buyers pay no stamp duty on properties valued up to $600,000, with a partial concession for properties between $600,001 and $750,000. Above $750,000, full rates apply.</p>
    <p>Victoria also offers an off-the-plan concession: when buying an apartment off the plan, stamp duty is calculated on the land value only at the contract date rather than the full completed property value. This concession has been extended and eligible apartments see significantly reduced duty calculated on land value only.</p>
    <p>If you are weighing up renting versus buying in Melbourne, CalcPhi's <a href="/australia/rent-vs-buy-calculator/">Rent vs Buy Calculator</a> factors stamp duty into the full upfront cost comparison.</p>


    <h2>Queensland</h2>
    <p>Queensland is generally the most affordable state for stamp duty on mid-range properties. The standard transfer duty scale starts at 1% for the first $5,000 and rises to 5.75% for the portion above $1,000,000. Stamp duty on a $600,000 property in QLD is approximately $15,925, compared to $22,490 in NSW and $31,070 in Victoria.</p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Purchase Price</th>
          <th>Standard Duty (approx.)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$15,925</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$600,000</td><td>$21,850</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$750,000</td><td>$27,600</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$900,000</td><td>$37,350</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$1,000,000</td><td>$38,025</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>Queensland's home concession rate applies when you purchase your principal place of residence and move in within 12 months, reducing the effective rate for owner-occupiers. For first home buyers, there is a full rebate on established homes up to $700,000 and zero stamp duty on new builds with no price cap — making it one of the most generous new-build concessions in the country. Queensland also offers a $30,000 First Home Owner Grant for new homes under $750,000, which can be stacked on top of the duty savings.</p>


    <h2>Western Australia</h2>
    <p>Western Australia uses a sliding transfer duty scale with the lowest top marginal stamp duty rate in Australia at 5.15%, with the scale starting at 1.9% for lower-value properties.</p>

    <table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Purchase Price</th>
          <th>Standard Duty (approx.)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>$400,000</td><td>$11,115</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$500,000</td><td>$17,765</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$700,000</td><td>$26,740</td></tr>
        <tr><td>$900,000</td><td>$37,840</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>WA provides a transfer duty exemption for first home buyers on purchases up to $430,000, with concessions available up to $530,000. Perth's median house price has hit record highs as interstate migration continues — meaning most metropolitan suburbs now trade above the FHB threshold, and many first home buyers no longer qualify for the full exemption.</p>


    <h2>South Australia</h2>
    <p>South Australia charges stamp duty on a sliding scale from 1% to 5.5%, placing it broadly in the mid-range nationally. On a $600,000 SA property, standard duty is approximately $26,830.</p>
    <p>The standout provision for first home buyers is that, since June 2024, first home buyers purchasing a new home in SA pay zero stamp duty with no price cap — a policy that significantly reduces the cost of entering the property market through a new build.</p>


    <h2>Tasmania</h2>
    <p>Tasmania applies transfer duty at progressive rates. Tasmania offers a full stamp duty exemption for first home buyers purchasing any property (new or established) valued up to $750,000, extended through to June 2026. A 50% duty discount also applies for all owner-occupied purchases up to $750,000, giving even non-first home buyers meaningful savings.</p>
    <p>Foreign buyers in Tasmania pay an 8% surcharge on top of standard duty rates.</p>


    <h2>Australian Capital Territory</h2>
    <p>The ACT operates the most structurally different stamp duty regime in Australia. The ACT uses a sliding scale with rates from 0% to 4.95% and has been transitioning to a land tax model, gradually replacing stamp duty for owner-occupiers. Standard duty on a $600,000 ACT property is approximately $20,450.</p>
    <p>First home buyers in the ACT can access a full stamp duty exemption through the Home Buyer Concession Scheme (HBCS) on properties up to $607,500, subject to an income test ($170,000 for singles, $227,000 for couples). The ACT also allows buyers to opt into an annual land tax payment in lieu of upfront duty — a mechanism it has offered longer than any other Australian jurisdiction.</p>


    <h2>Northern Territory</h2>
    <p>The Northern Territory applies transfer duty on a progressive scale, with amounts generally lower than southern states at equivalent property values. Standard duty on a $500,000 NT property is approximately $19,700.</p>
    <p>The NT provides a First Home Owner Discount that reduces stamp duty by up to $18,601 on eligible purchases, alongside a Principal Place of Residence rebate. House-and-land package concessions in the NT apply until June 2027. The NT and ACT are the only two Australian jurisdictions that do not impose a foreign buyer stamp duty surcharge.</p>


    <h2>Foreign Buyer Surcharges</h2>
    <p>If you are not an Australian citizen or permanent resident, you pay an additional surcharge on top of standard stamp duty. As of May 2026, surcharge rates are 9% in NSW, 8% in Victoria, 8% in Queensland, 8% in Tasmania, 7% in Western Australia, and 7% in South Australia. The ACT and NT charge no foreign buyer surcharge.</p>
    <p>On a $900,000 property in NSW, the surcharge alone is $81,000 — on top of the base stamp duty of approximately $35,835, bringing the total to roughly $116,835. Temporary visa holders, including those on 482 and student visas, are generally treated as foreign persons for stamp duty purposes.</p>


