    <h2>Worked Example: What Does Stamp Duty Actually Cost?</h2>
    <p>To make these numbers concrete, here is a real-world scenario comparing two buyers — Sarah and Marcus — who are purchasing at the same price in different states.</p>

    <h3>Sarah — First Home Buyer in New South Wales ($780,000 Purchase)</h3>
    <p>Sarah is buying her first home in Newcastle for $780,000. Because this is below NSW's $800,000 first home buyer exemption threshold, she pays <strong>zero stamp duty</strong>. Without the exemption, the standard duty on a $780,000 NSW property would be approximately $30,068. Sarah saves that entire amount by qualifying under the First Home Buyers Assistance Scheme.</p>
    <p>Sarah still needs to budget for other upfront costs — conveyancing, building inspection, and potentially Lenders Mortgage Insurance (LMI) if her deposit is under 20%. Use CalcPhi's <a href="/australia/lmi-calculator/">LMI Calculator</a> to estimate that cost and plan your full deposit strategy.</p>

    <h3>Marcus — Owner-Occupier in Victoria ($780,000 Purchase)</h3>
    <p>Marcus is buying his second home in Geelong for $780,000 and does not qualify for any first home buyer concession. Under Victoria's rate schedule, he pays the full standard duty. Using Victoria's progressive brackets — nil under $25,000, stepping up to 6.5% on the portion above $870,000 — Marcus's duty on $780,000 works out to approximately <strong>$41,740</strong>. That is over $41,000 more than Sarah pays for a property at the same price, purely because of the state and buyer status difference.</p>
    <p>Marcus budgets for this before taking out his mortgage. To see how stamp duty affects total borrowing capacity and monthly repayments, use CalcPhi's <a href="/australia/mortgage-calculator/">Mortgage Calculator</a> and <a href="/australia/borrowing-power-calculator/">Borrowing Power Calculator</a> together.</p>

    <h3>The key takeaway</h3>
    <p>The same $780,000 purchase results in a stamp duty bill of $0 in NSW (for a first home buyer) versus approximately $41,740 in Victoria (for a standard buyer). State of purchase and buyer status are the two most powerful variables in any stamp duty calculation. Always model your specific scenario before committing to a purchase — run your numbers in seconds with CalcPhi's free <a href="/australia/stamp-duty-calculator/">Stamp Duty Calculator</a>.</p>


    <h2>How to Reduce Your Stamp Duty Legally</h2>
    <p>Stamp duty is not fixed — there are several legitimate strategies to reduce or eliminate it depending on your circumstances.</p>
    <p>The most straightforward path is buying as a first home buyer below your state's exemption threshold. In NSW, that means staying under $800,000; in Victoria, under $600,000; in Queensland, under $700,000 for established homes (or building new with no price cap). If you are close to a threshold, a $10,000 reduction in the purchase price could save $20,000 or more in duty if it keeps you inside the exemption bracket.</p>
    <p>Buying a new build in South Australia or Queensland as a first home buyer avoids stamp duty entirely regardless of price. In Victoria, purchasing off the plan significantly reduces the duty base by limiting it to land value. In the ACT, opting for the annual land tax model instead of upfront duty reduces the immediate cash needed at settlement. Pensioner concessions exist in several states for eligible downsizers.</p>
    <p>Pair CalcPhi's <a href="/australia/stamp-duty-calculator/">Stamp Duty Calculator</a> with the <a href="/australia/mortgage-calculator/">Mortgage Calculator</a> to model the full upfront cost picture — deposit, stamp duty, LMI, and ongoing repayments — before you sign a contract.</p>


