    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <ul class="faq-list">
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">How is stamp duty calculated in Australia?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Stamp duty is calculated using a tiered bracket system applied to the property's purchase price or market value, whichever is higher. Only the portion of value within each bracket is taxed at that bracket's rate — similar to how income tax brackets work. The total duty is the sum of tax applied across all applicable brackets. Rates and bracket thresholds are set separately by each state and territory government and differ considerably across jurisdictions.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Which Australian state has the lowest stamp duty?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Queensland generally has the lowest stamp duty for mid-range properties. Stamp duty on a $600,000 property is approximately $15,925 in QLD, compared to $22,490 in NSW and $31,070 in VIC. For higher-value purchases, WA is often the cheapest thanks to its top marginal rate of just 5.15% — the lowest in the country.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Do first home buyers pay stamp duty in Australia?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Most first home buyers pay reduced or zero stamp duty in 2026. Full exemptions apply up to $800,000 in NSW, $600,000 in Victoria, and $700,000 for established homes in Queensland — with no price cap at all on new builds in QLD and SA. Thresholds, income tests, and eligibility conditions vary by state, so always confirm your entitlement with the relevant state revenue office before committing to a purchase.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">When is stamp duty paid in Australia?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Stamp duty is due within 30 days of settlement in most states and territories. Your conveyancer or solicitor calculates the amount and coordinates payment as part of the settlement process. Late payment can attract interest and penalties from the relevant state revenue authority. In NSW and the ACT, eligible buyers may be able to opt for an annual land tax in lieu of a lump-sum payment at settlement.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">What is the foreign buyer stamp duty surcharge in Australia?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Foreign buyers pay an additional surcharge on top of standard stamp duty when purchasing residential property in Australia. As of May 2026, surcharge rates are 9% in NSW, 8% in Victoria, Queensland, and Tasmania, and 7% in Western Australia and South Australia. The ACT and NT are the only jurisdictions with no foreign buyer surcharge. Temporary visa holders — including those on 482, student, and other temporary visas — are generally treated as foreign persons for duty purposes.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Is stamp duty tax-deductible in Australia?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Stamp duty on a principal place of residence is not tax-deductible. For investment properties, however, it forms part of the property's cost base and can reduce your capital gains tax liability when you eventually sell — it raises the base cost, which reduces the taxable gain at disposal. It cannot be immediately deducted against rental income in the year of purchase. Always confirm your specific position with a registered tax agent. For a clearer picture of CGT exposure on an investment property, use CalcPhi's <a href="/australia/capital-gains-tax-calculator/">Capital Gains Tax Calculator</a>.</p>
      </li>
      <li class="faq-item">
        <h3 class="faq-q">Can stamp duty be added to a home loan in Australia?</h3>
        <p class="faq-a">Stamp duty cannot generally be added directly to your mortgage in Australia. Lenders require it to be paid as an upfront cost at settlement from your own funds. In most states, it must be settled within 30 days of the property transaction completing. This makes it a critical item to save for separately alongside your deposit. Some lenders may factor it into their overall assessment of your liquid savings when evaluating your loan application, which is another reason to budget for it well in advance.</p>
      </li>
    </ul>

